// diag_folded_source_text_reject: an overflow diagnostic on a CONSTANT
// EXPRESSION must name the expression, not only the value it folded to.
//
// `var x int8 = 100 + 100` reported `constant 200 overflows int8`. The 200
// appears nowhere in the source, so the message gave no way to find which
// expression was at fault — the more operands involved, the worse it got.
// Go names both: `cannot use 100 + 100 (untyped int constant 200) as int8
// value in variable declaration (overflows)`.
//
// The unfolded AST is available at the diagnostic site (`top` in
// adapt_untyped_int_operand, expression_checker.c) — it is the node the fold
// ran ON — so the expression is rendered back to text the way Go's own
// diagnostics do, with a printer over the AST rather than by slicing the
// source buffer.
//
// The substring `overflows int8` is deliberately preserved so the many
// sibling sidecars that match on `overflows` keep working, and the
// single-literal path (`var x int8 = 200`, check_literal_range) is
// deliberately NOT changed: there the rendered text and the folded value
// are the same string, so naming both would only add noise.
//
// The rendering is the expression AS PARSED, not a byte-exact source slice:
// a nested binary operand is parenthesised (`(10 * 10) + 100`) and integer
// literals print in decimal, since the parser normalises them and the
// original spelling is not retained (`0xFF + 1` renders `255 + 1`). Both
// are documented on ast_expr_to_source.
package main

func main() {
	var x int8 = 100 + 100
	_ = x
}
